From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MrLGamer <mrlgam3r@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 4 Sep 2026 14:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] amdhip64_7: Add a bridge from the Windows HIP runtime to host
 ROCm

Windows HIP applications load amdhip64_7.dll (the ROCm 7 runtime shipped
with Adrenalin drivers), which has no Wine counterpart. Forward the
runtime API to the host libamdhip64.so.7 through a unixlib: HIP fat
binaries are clang offload bundles of amdgcn-amd-amdhsa code objects,
identical on Windows and Linux, so registration data, kernel handles and
device pointers pass through unmodified and kernels run natively on the
host GPU. The DLL fails to load when no host runtime is found, matching
the Windows behavior without an AMD driver.

For D3D12<->HIP zero-copy interop (e.g. DLSS-NR): ROCm on Linux reports
no adapter LUID, so apps that match the HIP device to the D3D12 adapter
by LUID refuse to share; the bridge stamps the DXGI adapter LUID into
the device properties (auto-detected, PROTON_HIP_LUID overrides) so the
match succeeds. The runtime is loaded from a bundled <build>/files/lib/rocm
directory when present, so no container filesystem shares are needed. Shared D3D12/D3D11
handle-type imports are resolved to the backing dmabuf fd (via the win32u
d3dkmt helpers, the same path winevulkan uses on import) and re-imported
as opaque fds, which the host runtime accepts; failures fall through so
callers can use their staging path. PROTON_HIP_LIB overrides the library
path and PROTON_HIP_PRELOAD preloads host deps the Steam Runtime lacks
(e.g. libnuma). win32u exports the two d3dkmt fd helpers for the bridge.
---
